/**
\page MITKWorkbenchManualPage The MITK Workbench

\tableofcontents

Welcome to the basic MITK user manual. This document gives a concise overview of the basic functions of MITK and instructions how to use them.

\section MITKWorkbenchManualPage_UserInterface The User Interface

The layout of the MITK applications is designed to give a clear distinction between the different work areas. The following figure gives an overview of the main sections of the user interface.

\imageMacro{MITKUserManual_GUICommented.png,"The Common MITK Application Graphical User Interface",16.00}

\section MITKWorkbenchManualPage_Menu Menu

\subsection MITKWorkbenchManualPage_Menu_File File

This dialogue allows you to save, load and clear entire projects. This includes any nodes in the data manager.

\subsection MITKWorkbenchManualPage_Menu_Edit Edit

This dialogue supports undo and redo operations.

\subsection MITKWorkbenchManualPage_Menu_Window Window

The "Show View" menu opens a specific \ref MITKWorkbenchManualPage_Views "view", which can then be moved within the working area according to your wishes.
"Reset Layout" restores the default arrangement of the views.

The Preferences dialogue allows you to adjust and save your general and view-specific custom settings.

\imageMacro{MITKUserManual_WindowDropdown.png,"Window dropdown menu",4.89}

\subsection MITKWorkbenchManualPage_Menu_Help Help

This dialogue contains the user manuals view with the view-specific documentation, the welcome screen and information about MITK.
"Diagnostic Data..." opens the list of saved crash dumps and diagnostic snapshots (see \ref MITKWorkbenchManualPage_CrashDumpDialog).

\section MITKWorkbenchManualPage_Views Views

Each solution for a specific problem is realized as a self contained view. Thus you can create a workflow for your problem by combining the capabilities of different views to suit your needs within the working area.

By pressing and holding the left mouse button on a view tab you can move it around to suit your needs, even out of the application window.

\section MITKWorkbenchManualPage_SystemLoadIndicator System Load Indicator/Memory Usage

The System Load Indicator in the lower right hand corner of the screen gives information about the memory currently required by the MITK application.
Keep in mind that image processing is a highly memory intensive task and monitor the indicator to avoid your system freezing while constantly swapping to the hard drive.

\section MITKWorkbenchManualPage_DataManager Data Manager
The Data Manager is the central component of MITK to manage medical data like images, segmentation masks, registrations, surfaces, point sets, annotations and measurements.
For a detailed description of the Data Manager please be referred to the view documentation:   \ref org_mitk_views_datamanager.

\section MITKWorkbenchManualPage_ImageNavigator Image Navigator
Fast movement through the available data can be achieved by using the Image Navigator. By moving the sliders around you can scroll through the slides and timesteps.
For a detailed description of the Image Navigator please be referred to the Image Navigator view documentation:   \ref org_mitk_views_imagenavigator.

\section MITKWorkbenchManualPage_FourWindowView Standard Display

The four window view of the standard display is the heart of the MITK image viewing. For a detailed description of the four window view and the levelwindow please be referred to the standard display view documentation:   \ref org_mitk_editors_stdmultiwidget.

\section MITKWorkbenchManualPage_CrashDumpDialog Diagnostic Data After a Crash

If MITK closed unexpectedly, or had to be terminated while it was not responding, a diagnostic snapshot (crash dump) of that session is saved on your computer. On the next start, a dialog lists the saved dumps with their kind and the MITK version that produced them, and asks what to do with them:

<ul>
  <li><b>Keep for later</b> keeps the dumps, so you can hand them in with a problem report. They can be found later under <i>Help > Diagnostic Data...</i>.</li>
  <li><b>Remove</b> (the default, also chosen when you simply close the dialog) deletes them immediately.</li>
  <li><b>File report...</b> appears only when a problem-report feature is installed. It keeps the dumps and passes them on to that feature.</li>
</ul>

Either way, the dialog does not show these dumps again.

A crash dump contains parts of the application's memory from the crashed session. If at any time during that session you opened, browsed or queried data about real people that was not fully anonymized (including in the DICOM browser or a PACS query), the dump may contain such data and must be handled through your usual process for patient or study-participant data. Other rules of your organisation may also restrict sharing a dump, for example for confidential or unpublished data. Please check before you pass a dump on, and send one to the MITK developers only if you are permitted to share it. MITK never uploads crash dumps; they stay on your computer until you delete them or pass them on yourself.

\subsection MITKWorkbenchManualPage_CrashDumpDialog_Manager Diagnostic Data

<i>Help > Diagnostic Data...</i> lists all saved dumps: crash dumps, dumps of terminated unresponsive sessions, and snapshots captured with "Capture diagnostics" from an error message. For each dump it shows when it was written, its size, the MITK version, whether a copy of that session's log file was kept with it, and whether it is new or was already shown. You can show a dump in the file manager, open its log, copy its path, and delete selected or all dumps. Deleting cannot be undone.

\subsection MITKWorkbenchManualPage_CrashDumpDialog_Preferences Preferences

The page <i>Reports and Diagnostics > Crash Dumps</i> in the preferences controls:

<ul>
  <li><b>Record crash dumps</b> - whether dumps are written at all.</li>
  <li><b>Dumps kept per kind</b> - how many of the most recent dumps of each kind are kept (1 to 10). A dump's copy of its session log is deleted together with it.</li>
  <li><b>Watchdog for unresponsive sessions</b> - takes a snapshot when the user interface stops responding for the given time. The snapshot is deleted again if MITK recovers and is kept only if MITK has to be terminated. Not available on macOS.</li>
</ul>

The settings take effect at the next start and apply to every installation of the application on this computer, since all of them share one folder for diagnostic data.

If you do not want MITK to write crash dumps for a session, start the application with the command-line option <tt>\--MITK.no-crash-dumps</tt> or set the environment variable <tt>MITK_NO_CRASH_DUMPS</tt>. Both take precedence over the preference, and existing dumps can still be handled under <i>Help > Diagnostic Data...</i>.

*/
